course: dictionary
lesson: 15906
title: Words that dodge the subject
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes people feel shy about a plain word, so they use a softer or funnier one instead. This is called a euphemism. Context shows you when it is happening. «Mum hung the unmentionables at the back of the washing line, where nobody could see them.» Hiding them at the back is a clue that these are things people feel shy about.
explain: Unmentionables is a noun, and it is only used in the plural, with an s. It means underwear: pants, knickers and bras. The word is old-fashioned and a bit jokey, because it pretends underwear is too rude to name. It is used in every English-speaking country, so you will meet it in stories from all over the world.
explain: The adjective unmentionable is different. «The smell from the old bin was unmentionable.» Here it means too shocking or embarrassing to talk about. The clues are the smell and the way the sentence sounds: nobody wants to describe it. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Unmentionables means underwear, such as pants, knickers and bras.
fact: Unmentionables is a noun that is only used in the plural, with an s.
fact: A euphemism is a softer or funnier word used in place of a plain one.
fact: Unmentionable is an adjective meaning too shocking or embarrassing to talk about.
fact: Unmentionables is an old-fashioned, humorous word used in all English-speaking countries.
exercise: «Mum hung the unmentionables at the back of the washing line.» What are unmentionables here? | *Underwear | Towels for the bath | Garden tools | Dusty curtains
exercise: «The smell from the old bin was unmentionable.» What does unmentionable mean? | Easy to see from far away | Old and made of wood | *Too shocking or embarrassing to talk about | Very pleasant and sweet
exercise: «Mum hung the unmentionables at the back of the washing line.» Find and copy the word that is a euphemism for underwear. | *unmentionables | line | washing | back
test: Why might a writer choose the word unmentionables for underwear? | To talk about the weather | To describe a dangerous storm | To name a kind of fruit | *To joke about underwear, which people feel shy about
test: Which spelling is the correct plural noun? | *unmentionables | unmention | unmentionabling | unmentionable
exam: In «Nobody would discuss the unmentionable topic at dinner», what does unmentionable mean? | *Too embarrassing to talk about | Very tiny | Loudly sung | Delicious
